      en: "Kranji Marshes is Singapore's largest freshwater marshland, spanning 56.8 hectares in the northwestern part of the island. Opened in 2016 after a three-year restoration project, it was converted from former fish ponds into a thriving wetland ecosystem. The marshes serve as a critical stopover for migratory birds along the East Asian-Australasian Flyway, with over 170 species recorded. Key conservation efforts include the installation of a 4.3 km nature trail and a 11-meter-high Raptor Tower for birdwatching. The site is also home to rare flora such as the Common Bladderwort (Utricularia aurea) and the Yellow Flag Iris (Iris pseudacorus). Educational programs emphasize the importance of freshwater wetlands in urban environments.",
